---
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
import CrossSell from '../../components/CrossSell.astro';
---
<AppShell mode="public" title="Fleet guide — LoopSkill Docs"
     description="Create a fleet, subscribe bundles, distribute fleet keys, and keep multiple agents in sync. Free to create; Pro raises the member-key cap.">
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-12">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Documentation · Fleet</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">Fleet: multi-agent skill sync.</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A <strong>fleet</strong> is a named group of agents that all share the same bundle.
        You create the fleet once, subscribe one or more bundles to it, distribute the fleet
        key to each agent, and from then on all agents stay in sync with a single
        <code>loopskill_fleet_sync</code> call.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Tier requirement:</strong> Creating a fleet is free — any signed-in account can create a
        fleet and one fleet member key. Pro ($9.95/mo) raises the member-key cap so you can distribute
        the fleet key to more agents. Fleet keys (<code>rec_fleet_*</code>) are separate from standard
        API keys and are scoped to specific bundles.
      </p>

      <h2>Concepts</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fleet</strong> — a named group with a shared fleet key.
          Created by calling <code>loopskill_fleet_create</code>.
          Fleet key format: <code>rec_fleet_&lt;8hex&gt;_&lt;32hex&gt;</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Subscription</strong> — links a fleet to a bundle on a channel
          (<code>stable</code> or <code>latest</code>). Created by calling
          <code>loopskill_fleet_subscribe</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sync</strong> — updates all agents in the fleet to the subscribed bundle's
          current pinned versions. Called per-agent via <code>loopskill_fleet_sync</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fleet key</strong> — the plaintext key shown ONCE on creation. Store it
          securely (e.g. in your secret manager). It is NOT re-showable — if lost, create a
          new fleet.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 1 — Create a bundle</h2>
      <p>
        Fleets sync from bundles. Create one first if you don't have one:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`// Via MCP (from your agent):
loopskill_list_bundle({})   // list existing bundles

// Via API:
curl -X POST -H "x-api-key: $RECIPES_API_KEY" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"name": "Production Fleet Bundle"}' \\
  https://app.loopskill.io/api/cookbooks`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Add skills to the bundle via the dashboard or the
        <code>POST /api/cookbooks/{`{id}`}/skills</code> endpoint.
        Each skill is pinned at the current latest version when added.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Create a fleet</h2>
      <p>
        From inside your agent conversation:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`loopskill_fleet_create({ name: "Production Fleet" })`}</code></pre>
      <p>Response:</p>
      <pre><code>{`{
  "fleet_id": "uuid...",
  "fleet_key": "rec_fleet_a1b2c3d4_e5f6...",
  "name": "Production Fleet"
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <strong>Save the <code>fleet_key</code> immediately.</strong> It is shown only once.
        The server stores only a sha256 hash of the key — there is no way to recover it.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Subscribe a bundle to the fleet</h2>
      <pre><code>{`loopskill_fleet_subscribe({
  fleet_id: "uuid...",
  cookbook_id: "uuid...",
  channel: "stable"   // "stable" (default) or "latest"
})`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The <code>channel</code> controls which bundle skills the fleet syncs:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>stable</strong> — syncs only the pinned, reviewed versions in the bundle.</li>
        <li><strong>latest</strong> — syncs to the most recently published version of each skill,
            even if the bundle pin hasn't been updated. Use with care in production.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 4 — Distribute the fleet key</h2>
      <p>
        Each agent that should be part of the fleet needs the fleet key as an environment variable:
      </p>
      <pre><code>export LOOPSKILL_API_KEY=rec_fleet_a1b2c3d4_e5f6...</code></pre>
      <p>
        Fleet keys are bundle-scoped. An agent using a fleet key can only install skills that
        are in the fleet's subscribed bundles — it cannot browse or install from the general catalog.
        This is the security boundary that makes fleet keys safe to distribute broadly.
      </p>
      <p>Distribute via your preferred secret management approach:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>AWS Secrets Manager / Parameter Store</li>
        <li>HashiCorp Vault</li>
        <li>GitHub Actions secrets (for CI agents)</li>
        <li>A shared <code>.env</code> file on a private host (acceptable for single-machine fleets)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 5 — Sync agents</h2>
      <p>
        On each agent machine, trigger a sync via the MCP tool (the standalone
        <code>loopskill</code> CLI does not have a <code>sync</code> subcommand today —
        it ships <code>import</code>/<code>diff</code>/<code>pull</code>/<code>apply</code> only):
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`// Via MCP (from any agent in the fleet):
loopskill_fleet_sync({ fleet_id: "uuid...", dry_run: false })`}</code></pre>
      <p>The sync response shows what changed:</p>
      <pre><code>{`{
  "fleet_id": "uuid...",
  "cookbooks_synced": [
    {
      "cookbook_id": "uuid...",
      "changes": [
        {"slug": "seo-audit-engine", "from": "1.2.0", "to": "1.3.0", "action": "updated"},
        {"slug": "super-memory", "from": null, "to": "1.4.2", "action": "installed"}
      ],
      "applied": true
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Pass <code>dry_run: true</code> to preview changes without applying them.
      </p>

      <h2>Listing fleets</h2>
      <pre><code>{`loopskill_fleet_list({})
// Returns:
{
  "fleets": [
    {
      "fleet_id": "uuid...",
      "name": "Production Fleet",
      "subscriptions": [
        {"cookbook_id": "uuid...", "channel": "stable"}
      ]
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Automation pattern: nightly sync</h2>
      <p>
        For unattended agents (CI runners, always-on servers), the sync loop runs via the
        MCP tool or meta-skill — there is no standalone CLI <code>sync</code> subcommand today
        (the published <code>loopskill</code> CLI ships <code>import</code>/<code>diff</code>/
        <code>pull</code>/<code>apply</code> only). Schedule an agent invocation that calls
        <code>loopskill_fleet_sync</code> from your cron or systemd timer, e.g. a headless
        agent run that logs to <code>/var/log/loopskill-sync.log</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`# crontab entry (runs daily at 05:30 UTC)
30 5 * * * LOOPSKILL_API_KEY=rec_fleet_... /path/to/your/agent --prompt "call loopskill_fleet_sync" >> /var/log/loopskill-sync.log 2>&1`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Security considerations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fleet keys are long-lived.</strong> Rotate them if an agent is decommissioned
          or a key leaks. Create a new fleet (or contact support to re-key an existing one).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bundle-scoped.</strong> A fleet key cannot install skills outside its
          subscribed bundles. This bounds the blast radius of a compromised key.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No catalog browsing.</strong> Agents with fleet keys cannot call
          <code>/api/skills/search</code> for general browsing — only
          <code>/api/skills/install</code> for allowed slugs.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related docs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/security">Security model</a> — rec_fleet key auth details</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/api-reference">API reference</a> — bundle endpoints</li>
        <li><a href="/integrations">Integrations</a> — per-agent MCP config</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <CrossSell />
  </section>
</AppShell>
